# Azure Cloud-Native .NET Microservices Platform

An end-to-end cloud-native engineering project focused on designing, deploying, securing, and operating .NET microservices on Microsoft Azure.

The goal is to build a reproducible platform that demonstrates practical skills in cloud infrastructure, Kubernetes, Infrastructure as Code, distributed systems, resilience, observability, and CI/CD.

> **Project status:** Initial setup and architecture planning.

## Objectives

Build and operate a cloud-native .NET microservices application while developing practical experience in:

- Microsoft Azure infrastructure and services
- Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)
- Terraform and Infrastructure as Code
- Containerization and Kubernetes operations
- Distributed systems and microservice communication
- Application and infrastructure resilience
- OpenTelemetry-based observability
- Identity, access management, and secrets management
- CI/CD automation with GitHub Actions
- Troubleshooting, performance, and operational practices

## Application

The project uses Microsoft's [eShop](https://github.com/ibraheemcisse/eShop) as its application foundation.

The application will serve as a practical workload for exploring cloud-native architecture and platform engineering rather than spending project time building a business application from scratch.

## Planned Engineering Areas

| Area | Planned work |
|---|---|
| Application | Understand and prepare the .NET microservices application |
| Infrastructure | Design and provision Azure resources with Terraform |
| Containers | Build and manage container images |
| Kubernetes | Deploy and operate workloads on AKS |
| Networking | Explore ingress, service communication, and network security |
| Security | Apply least privilege, identity, and secrets management |
| Resilience | Implement and test failure-handling strategies |
| Observability | Collect and investigate logs, metrics, and traces |
| CI/CD | Automate build, test, and deployment workflows |
| Operations | Test failures, troubleshoot incidents, and document recovery |

These are planned capabilities, not claims of completed implementation.

